# Lambda vs Kappa

<PageMeta level="advanced" time="8 min" prereq={[['Batch vs streaming', '/docs/flink/foundations/batch-vs-streaming']]} />

<Objectives>

- Describe both architectures and the problem each was invented to solve
- Explain why Lambda fell out of favour, specifically
- Recognise the hybrid that most companies actually run, and why that is fine

</Objectives>

## Lambda

Two parallel paths over the same data: a fast, approximate one and a slow, correct one.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    S[(Event log)] --> B[Batch layer<br/>reprocess everything nightly]
    S --> SP[Speed layer<br/>streaming, approximate]
    B --> BV[(Batch views<br/>authoritative)]
    SP --> RV[(Real-time views<br/>recent only)]
    BV --> Q[Serving layer<br/>merge both]
    RV --> Q
    Q --> U[Query]
```

The argument in 2012 was sound: streaming systems of the day could not give you
correctness, so you used them for freshness and let a nightly batch job produce the
truth. The serving layer merged "everything up to last night" with "since last night".

### Why it fell out of favour

Not because the idea was wrong, but because of one practical cost that never went away:

<Callout type="key">

**You write every piece of business logic twice**, in two systems, with two sets of
semantics — and the numbers never quite agree.

Then you spend a meaningful fraction of your team's time reconciling them. Every new
metric doubles that work. Every bug fix has to be applied in two places and verified
against two engines that round, order and handle nulls differently.

</Callout>

Secondary costs: two systems to operate, two on-call rotations, two skill sets, and a
serving layer whose merge logic is itself a source of subtle bugs at the boundary.

## Kappa

One path. Streaming only. Reprocessing means replaying the log through the same code.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    S[(Event log<br/>long retention)] --> F[Stream processing<br/>ONE codebase]
    F --> V[(Views)]
    V --> Q[Query]
    S -.->|replay from offset 0<br/>into a NEW job| F2[Stream processing v2]
    F2 -.-> V2[(New views)]
    V2 -.->|swap when caught up| Q
```

The insight: if your stream processor is correct and your log is durable, "batch" is
just "streaming over old data". You do not need a second system — you need a second
*run*.

### What Kappa requires to be real

It is not free. It works only if all of these hold:

| Requirement | Why |
| --- | --- |
| **Long log retention** | To reprocess a year, you need a year of log. Tiered storage makes this affordable. |
| **Event-time correctness** | A replay must produce the same result as the live run. This is [Level 2](/docs/flink/time/three-clocks) and it is non-negotiable. |
| **Bounded backfill resources** | Replaying a year in an hour is a very different resource profile from live traffic. [Watermark alignment](/docs/flink/watermarks/propagation-and-idleness) is essential here. |
| **Versioned, swappable outputs** | You run v2 alongside v1 and cut over when it catches up. |
| **Deterministic logic** | Same as [exactly-once requirement 3](/docs/flink/fault-tolerance/exactly-once). |

<Callout type="mistake">

Adopting Kappa while using processing-time windows.

A replay then produces completely different results from the live run, so the whole
premise — reprocessing yields the same answer — is false. You have one codebase and two
sets of numbers, which is worse than Lambda.

Kappa is downstream of event-time correctness. Get that right first.

</Callout>

## What most companies actually run

Neither, exactly. The common shape in 2026:

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    K[(Kafka<br/>7-30 day retention)] --> F["Flink<br/>streaming ingest + light transform"]
    F --> L[(Lakehouse table<br/>Iceberg / Delta / Paimon)]
    F --> RT[(Serving store<br/>low-latency views)]
    L --> BQ["Batch / ad-hoc SQL<br/>Trino, Spark, Athena"]
    L -.->|backfill: read the table,<br/>not the log| F2["Flink, BATCH mode<br/>SAME code"]
    F2 -.-> L
```

Streaming for **ingestion and low-latency views**; the lakehouse table as the durable,
queryable history; batch engines for ad-hoc analysis; and backfills run as the *same
Flink code in `BATCH` mode* over the table rather than over the Kafka log.

<Callout type="prod" title="Why this hybrid wins in practice">

It takes the good part of Kappa — **one codebase**, because Flink's unified API runs the
same job in streaming and batch mode — without requiring years of Kafka retention.

The lakehouse table becomes the replay source. That is cheaper than a very long Kafka
retention, it is directly queryable by everyone else in the company, and it gives you
time travel for free.

This is exactly the architecture [StreamForge](/docs/intro) packages: Kafka in, Flink
compiles the pipeline, query-ready Parquet lands in S3, and the catalog is kept in sync
so the data is immediately queryable.

</Callout>

## Choosing

| Situation | Architecture |
| --- | --- |
| Greenfield, event-time correct, moderate history | **Kappa** — one system, replay from the log |
| Existing large batch estate, streaming being added | **Hybrid** — stream ingest, batch analysis, unified backfill |
| Years of history, expensive to retain in Kafka | **Hybrid** — lakehouse table as the replay source |
| Regulatory requirement for an independently computed reconciliation | **Lambda-ish** — deliberately, and accept the double implementation |
| Latency requirement of minutes, no streaming skills on the team | Batch. Do not build any of this. |

That last row is real advice. The cheapest streaming architecture is the one you did not
need to build.

<Expert>

**Reprocessing is the real design constraint.** Whichever label you pick, the question
that determines your architecture is: *"When we find a bug in a metric, how do we
recompute the last six months?"*

If the answer involves writing new code in a different system, you have Lambda's cost
whether or not you have its diagram.

**Backfill throughput is not live throughput.** A backfill reads as fast as storage
allows, which can be 50× live rate. That changes everything: watermarks race, state
explodes, TTL does not expire (it is processing-time based), and sinks get hammered.
Plan backfills as a separate operational mode with their own resource profile and
alignment settings.

**Blue/green output tables.** The clean cutover pattern: v2 writes to
`metrics_v2`, you compare it against `metrics_v1` over an overlap window, then swap a
view or alias. This makes reprocessing a routine operation rather than an event, which
is the actual goal of all of this.

**Streaming lakehouse formats change the calculus.** Iceberg, Delta and Paimon accept
streaming upserts and deletes *and* serve batch queries. That is what made the hybrid
practical — before them, streaming into a queryable table meant either small-file chaos
or a separate serving database.

</Expert>

<Callout type="remember">

Lambda's cost is writing everything twice. Kappa's requirement is event-time correctness
plus enough retention. Most companies run the hybrid — stream in, lakehouse as history,
one codebase, backfills in batch mode.

</Callout>

